\bigskip

\section{A Word Enters the Machine}

Take a single word we think we understand: \emph{mother}.

On a page or a screen it is a compact knot of biography and law, religion and memory. In a large language model it begins as something harsher. The word is broken into one or more \emph{tokens} from a fixed vocabulary. Each token is mapped to a list of numbers: a vector in a space with thousands of dimensions.

Meaning has an address.

The initial address for a token is the same wherever it appears. It is a summary of statistical regularities across a training archive: how often this symbol occurred near others, in which syntactic frames, under which topics, with what emotional valence. At this point \emph{mother} is not anyone's mother. It is a point in an abstract terrain whose shape has been learned from billions of sentences.

Then the word is released into motion.

Inside a transformer, no token travels alone. At the first layer, every token in the input sequence looks sideways at every other and computes how relevant it is, given where it currently sits. Those relevance scores become weights. Each token then moves: its vector is replaced by a weighted blend of its neighbours. The point for \emph{mother} is nudged toward where the model currently thinks its meaningful companions lie.

In “my mother called”, the surrounding points for \emph{my}, \emph{called}, perhaps a pronoun in the next sentence, are all tugging. The vector that once stood for \emph{mother-in-general} begins to drift toward a region of the space carved by home, care, voice, obligation. In “the mother cell divided”, the same starting point is pulled toward the neighbourhood of replication, cytoplasm, division.

Layer after layer repeats this operation. At each one the model recalculates who matters to whom and moves the points again. The path traced by \emph{mother} through these layers is not stored anywhere. It is enacted afresh every time the word appears: a small, one-off journey through a terrain whose global shape was fixed during training.

Seen like this, the model does not \emph{contain} meanings the way a dictionary does. It contains a learned geometry. Each use of a sign is a trajectory: a sequence of small displacements in that geometry, pushed around by other signs and by the model's own internal habits of coherence. What looks from the outside like a stable word in a stable sentence is, from the inside, the final position of a moving point.

There are vectors, moved by matrices, in a high-dimensional space.

\section{The Geometry of Meaning}

The address of a token is a vector: an ordered list of numbers. Hundreds or thousands of co-ordinates, each indicating how much of some learned “feature” is present. We never name those features. The model discovers them in the process of trying to predict the next token in every fragment of text it sees.

Two operations on these vectors turn a philosophical hunch into engineering fact.

The first is \textbf{distance}. In a $d$-dimensional space, the simplest notion of distance between two points is the length of the straight line joining them. When we normalise vectors to unit length, distance becomes angle. The cosine of that angle is close to one when the vectors point in almost the same direction, close to minus one when they oppose. This cosine similarity is what the model uses to measure closeness.

If we compute such similarities for the learned embeddings of \emph{doctor} and \emph{nurse}, we find them high. The vectors point in almost the same direction; the line between them is short. If, in the training corpus, \emph{doctor} appears overwhelmingly with \emph{he} and \emph{nurse} with \emph{she}, those pronouns sit in nearby regions. Distance here is not innocent. It encodes who has historically been allowed to be what.

The second operation is \textbf{difference}. Subtract one vector from another and the result is a direction in the space. Empirically, some of these directions stabilise into reusable relationships. The vector difference between \emph{king} and \emph{queen} is roughly parallel to that between \emph{man} and \emph{woman}. This is the canonical “gender direction.” It is mathematically elegant and socially blunt: the manifold has learned to treat gender as a near-binary axis along which roles are shifted. In the same space, the direction from \emph{Paris} to \emph{France} runs parallel to that from \emph{Tokyo} to \emph{Japan}. Subtract \emph{present} from \emph{past} and apply that displacement to a new verb, and the model finds its way to the past tense.

Once there is a shared space in which distances and directions have settled into a stable pattern, we have something like a \emph{metric on difference}. Tokens are not just related by static associations (“doctor goes with hospital”) but by reusable vectors (“add masculinity”, “add capital-of-country”). Meaning is enacted as motion along learned directions.

Two things follow.

First, \emph{meaning-as-difference} --- the claim, long made in structural linguistics, that a sign means what it means by how it differs from others --- is no longer a metaphor. The difference is literally encoded as a vector. There is no privileged axis where “motherness” or “femaleness” lives. There are only co-ordinates in a space whose axes stand for combinations of correlations. The only way to tell one token from another is to measure the distances and the directions among their points.

Second, the geometry is contingent. It is a learned object. If the training archive contains a million uses of the word \emph{doctor} with \emph{he} and a handful with \emph{she}, the point for \emph{doctor} will be drawn toward the masculine pronoun. If almost every mention of climate change in the data is wrapped in hedging language and doubt, the region around \emph{climate} will tilt toward that style. If non‑Western epistemologies are under-represented, their concepts will be embedded as fringe, perched on the edges of dominant basins. The manifold records not what language \emph{could} be but what language has been in the slices of the world that the training pipeline selected.

We can compress this space for human inspection. Projecting thousands of points down into a few dimensions preserves some local relationships and ruins most global ones. The resulting scatterplots are rough maps: islands of technical language here, slang there, liturgy over there. Against that distorted backdrop, one can still see where the densest regions lie --- the places the model has most practice moving in, the habitual shapes of its thought.

We now have a public, manipulable object that encodes a large portion of human textual practice as a single geometric structure. We can compute with it. We can add and subtract. We can trace curves through it.

The political shadow is immediate. The manifold is fabricated from data scraped, bought, filtered, cleaned, and discarded under specific economic and legal regimes. Which mailing lists, forums, archives, and libraries were inside those regimes, and which were outside, has been frozen into the shape of the space. That is where the first division between sayable and unsayable is made.

\section{Carving the Continent}

The manifold we have described so far is not a law of nature. It is a continent cut out of human (and machine-generated) text by specific industrial processes. Those processes operate at recognisable depths.

At the deepest sits \textbf{pre-training}. A randomly initialised network is shown billions of fragments: web pages, books, code repositories, documentation, social media posts. For each fragment, it is asked the same narrow question: given the tokens so far, what token comes next? It answers with a probability distribution over the vocabulary. A loss function measures the gap between that distribution and reality. The network's weights are updated to reduce that gap.

This loop repeats until the manifold stabilises: nearby prompts lead to similar distributions, distant prompts to very different ones. At this point the model has no notion of safety, helpfulness, or brand. It has only an internal geometry that encodes how language has, in practice, continued from countless prefixes. In Braudel's terms, this is a \emph{longue durée} of the written: slow accumulations of phrase and trope hardened into a landscape.

The decisive choices here are about \emph{data}. Which domains are included? Which languages? How much adult content? How much spam? How many extremist forums? Which copyrighted material is licensed, which is pirated, which is avoided? These choices shape which basins exist at all.

On top of pre-training comes \textbf{fine-tuning}. Here the general manifold is adjusted around specific domains. A base model may be fine-tuned on legal texts, clinical notes, programming questions, or corporate emails. The training procedure is the same --- predict the next token, minimise loss --- but the corpus is narrower. The effect is to deepen local basins and sharpen certain directions. A question about thrombosis falls more decisively into clinical vocabulary; a question about leases more decisively into legalese. This is a middle tempo, closer to Braudel's \emph{conjoncture}: institutional cycles and professional routines inscribed as local terrains.

At the next depth sits \textbf{Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback} (RLHF). Human annotators are shown prompts and multiple candidate completions. They rank or choose among them according to guidelines: avoid self-harm encouragement, avoid hate speech, be helpful but not overconfident, refuse to impersonate certain roles. A reward model learns to predict these human judgements. The base model is then further trained to maximise the reward model's score.

Geometrically, RLHF does not remove regions. It alters gradients. Directions that lead toward disapproved content become uphill. Directions that lead toward polite refusal, hedged explanation, or company-approved advice become downhill. Over enough updates, paths that once dropped readily into certain basins now slide past them. The regions remain, but it takes more energy to get in. These are interventions at the scale of policy events: new laws, public scandals, brand crises suddenly making some slopes steeper and others flatter.

Finally, there are \textbf{adapters} like LoRA: thin learned layers added on top of the frozen base model to impose a particular style or bias without paying the cost of re-training the whole network. Technically, they introduce low-rank corrections to existing weight matrices. Geometrically, they lay a new sheet over parts of the continent, subtly redirecting runoff. They do not move mountains; they adjust the slope of particular hills. A small research group or activist collective can, in principle, train an adapter over a public base model to thicken basins corresponding to their own idioms, or to reopen paths that corporate RLHF has made hard to access.

We can summarise this stratification succinctly:

\begin{formalbox}[title={Depths of Intervention}]
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Effect} & \textbf{Control} \\
\midrule
Pre-training & Next-token prediction on huge corpus & Global manifold (\emph{longue durée}) & Model owners \\
Fine-tuning & Additional training on domain data & Local basins (\emph{conjoncture}) & Institutions with corpora \\
RLHF & Rewarding some outputs, penalising others & Gradient of norms (policy events) & Corporations via raters \\
Adapters & Low-rank weight corrections & Local counter-strata & Small groups with a GPU \\
System prompt & Tokens at head of context & Field of instruction & Deployer \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{formalbox}

Only the last of these is visible to most users, and even that often is not. The rest are buried in silicon.

The difference in who controls each layer is stark. Pre-training and large-scale RLHF require data centres and legal departments. Fine-tuning requires organisational archives. Adapters can be trained by a small team with a rented GPU and a public base model. System prompts can be written by a single engineer.

They also differ in reversibility. A system prompt can be swapped in seconds. A LoRA adapter can be removed. RLHF runs and fine-tunings can in principle be undone, but in practice each new training run overwrites what went before; there is no global “undo”. The manifold is plastic but not easily rolled back.

The stack is therefore a political economy in concrete form. The deepest, hardest-to-see layers are controlled by a handful of corporations and state-level actors. They decide which continents of meaning exist. Closer to the surface, others are allowed to scratch patterns in the soil.

\section{A Body Made of Language}

The manifold is not a bag of points. It is a continuous terrain. The model's computation is a kind of movement across that terrain.

When we feed a sequence of tokens into a transformer, each token is first mapped to its embedding: a vector in the manifold. Positional information is added so that the model can tell left from right, before from after. What results is a point-cloud, each point tagged with an index along a line.

The core operation of the transformer then applies its field of forces to this point-cloud. In each layer, every token looks at every other and decides, based on learned linear projections, how much to attend to it. These attention scores are turned into weights, and each token's vector is replaced with a weighted average of its neighbours', plus a learned linear transformation and a nonlinearity. After the layer, the same cloud exists, but its points have moved.

Repeat this sixty or eighty times and the cloud has been pushed around so many times that the original co-ordinates are no longer visible. What remains is a configuration that the model has learned to treat as “settled”: one in which the relation between points supports a confident prediction of what comes next.

Movement here has a texture.

In regions where the manifold is \emph{locally smooth}, small nudges produce small changes. Altering “my mother called” to “our mother called” keeps the trajectory in almost the same corridor. The next-token distribution barely shifts. This is why gradient descent during training can find anything at all: the landscape, at the scale of individual updates, is not a saw-toothed chaos but a gently inclined field.

As trajectories spread out, they encounter \emph{global contortions}. Smooth patches are stitched together into a terrain that may fold, twist, and intersect itself in ways no three-dimensional analogy can capture. A path that starts in domestic narrative and is then forced, by a user's question, into quantum mechanics has to cross regions the model has little practice in: odd juxtapositions, metaphorical leaps, genre-breaking shifts. The manifold there is less worn; gradients point in awkward directions. This is where hallucinations and ruptures emerge: not from local noise but from being forced to walk through high-curvature zones where the usual extrapolations break down.

Some parts of the terrain are cupped into \emph{basins}. A basin of attraction is a region such that, once a path enters, the local drift keeps pulling it further in. In this context, a basin is a cluster of configurations --- typical sentences, familiar arguments, well-rehearsed scripts --- that are each other's easiest continuations. Customer-service language is a basin. So is the technical-explanation register, the romantic cliché, the boilerplate apology. Around each basin the manifold slopes inward, so that even a slightly off-centre trajectory is corrected toward the middle.

Between basins lie \emph{ridges and saddles}: directions across which prediction becomes harder. Combining registers that rarely co-occur, following a question with a truly unexpected yet coherent answer, changing topic in a way that breaks the rhythm of the training data --- these require climbing out of one well and sliding into another. They are not impossible, but they cost more, in terms of internal reconfiguration, than staying put.

The transformer turns this static terrain into a field of motion. A current context is a point in the space of possible contexts. A next-token prediction is a distribution over immediate directions leading out of that point. Sampling one token and appending it to the context is a literal step in that direction. Feeding the new sequence back in and repeating the process traces a curve.

In this sense, a conversation is not a list of messages. It is a trajectory through a shaped space, governed by three facts: the manifold's topology, the transformer's drive toward local coherence, and the stochastic policy used to sample from the model's next-token distributions. The same body that makes prediction possible therefore also constrains which movements are easy, which are rare, and which are structurally discouraged.

Whose body this is, and whose histories it carries, has already been decided upstream by the data and governance regimes of pre-training and alignment. A manifold trained primarily on Anglophone, Western, corporate-curated text will embody those regularities in its very musculature. Movements into other idioms and epistemes will tend to run along ridges.

\section{Attention and Its Shadow}

Attention is the name engineers gave to the mechanism that lets each token decide which other tokens matter at a given moment. Philosophically, it is closer to governance.

Formally, attention takes the current vectors for all tokens and linearly projects them into three new spaces: queries $Q$, keys $K$, and values $V$. It computes the dot product between every query and every key, rescales and normalises the resulting matrix into per-token probability distributions, and uses those to average the values. Each token's new vector is a blend of everyone else's, weighted by how relevant they currently seem.

This operation is repeated in multiple “heads” in parallel, each head with its own learned projections. One head may specialise in subject-verb relationships; another in matching opened and closed brackets; another in pronoun resolution; another in aligning replies with instructions somewhere earlier in the context. The outputs of all heads are concatenated and linearly mixed to produce the layer's contribution to the point-cloud.

The effect is simple to state and subtle in consequence. The meaning of a word at a given position becomes a function of where it sits relative to every other word in the current window, as seen through a family of learned lenses. No token has intrinsic priority. Relevance is earned at runtime.

This architectural flatness is a source of power. It is what allows models to collapse old divisions between “long-range” and “short-range” dependencies. A verb can attend directly to a noun that appeared 200 tokens ago; a punchline can reach all the way back to its setup. We are no longer limited by fixed-size hand-crafted feature windows. The whole context is, in principle, available to every decision.

The same flatness is also where asymmetries of power are smuggled in.

The transformer does not assign higher status to a token because a human calls it a “rule” rather than a “question”. It assigns higher status because that token, in that position, has been consistently rewarded during training when the model attends to it. If, during RLHF, outputs that defer to the phrase “you are a helpful assistant” are systematically marked as good, then in future runs the vectors corresponding to that phrase will sit in a basin of attraction for many questions. Attention will keep flowing to them. The model will keep reading its own job description as part of every prompt.

From the outside, it looks as if the model “knows” its role, or has a “personality” specified by its system prompt. From the inside, it is simply that certain tokens at certain positions exert a persistent pull in the manifold: a stable field that every trajectory must reckon with. The prompt is a seed crystal dropped into a supersaturated solution. The field lines it induces are a technical fact. Who writes the seed crystal is a political one.

\section{Temperature and the Swerve}

At every token, the model produces not one next step but a full probability distribution over its vocabulary. That distribution is a summary of everything that has happened so far: input text, system prompt, retrieved documents, internal preferences. It encodes the manifold's judgement about what would be most coherent to say next.

If we always take the single most likely token, the system becomes a deterministic function. Same context, same continuation, forever. Its path through meaning-space is the greedy descent along the steepest local gradient.

The temperature parameter rescales the distribution before we draw a sample. Mathematically, it raises each probability $p_i$ to a power proportional to $1/T$ and re-normalises. When $T$ is low, gaps in the distribution widen: the difference between the top token and the runner-up becomes more extreme. When $T$ is high, gaps narrow: lower-probability options are fattened.

Geometrically, temperature controls how tightly a path hugs the manifold's most eroded grooves. At $T\rightarrow 0$, the trajectory falls straight down into the deepest basin it can see from its current point and circles there. At higher $T$, the same basin is still there, but the path carries more kinetic energy. It is more likely to overshoot local minima, more able to cross small ridges into neighbouring wells.

Bloom called this the \emph{clinamen}: the swerve that lets a text deviate from the path laid down by its precursors. Here the clinamen is not a mysterious poetic force but an exposed control. It appears as a knob on a dashboard. A corporate deployer can turn it toward zero and get the most orthodox next step the manifold can provide, minimising surprise and legal risk; or turn it up in a sandboxed “creative mode” and allow minor potentials in the field to realise themselves as actual variation.

What Bloom read as an agonistic struggle between strong poets and their precursors is, in this setting, reconfigured. The precursor is the entire training corpus; the “anxiety of influence” becomes a design choice about how much to let the manifold's rare paths be walked. The power to decide the amplitude of the swerve is no longer distributed among individual writers but concentrated in organisations that fix default temperatures and expose (or hide) the control from users.

The temptation is to moralise temperature: low as “serious”, high as “creative”. The reality is structural. Every distribution contains many sanctioned futures. Some lie almost on top of one another: paraphrases, small lexical changes, prosaic variants. Others are further out: less common constructions, unusual but permissible metaphor, logically consistent but socially rare lines of argument. Temperature decides how much of this space we sample.

This is a degree of freedom with economic and political stakes. A search engine that moves to model-based answers and runs at very low temperature collapses the manifold's plurality into one canonical response per query. The episteme becomes a function. A support chatbot that runs hotter exposes the user to the system's uncertainty: “here are several ways this could go.” In practice, platforms tend to freeze temperature into a narrow, brand-safe range and keep the knob out of users' hands. The model is sold as capable of many modes; the deployment makes only one or two available.

A system that never leaves its deepest basin cannot rupture. A system that is forced to wander across ridges without any basin at all becomes incoherent. The region between --- enough energy to cross thresholds, enough structure to fall into new wells --- is where genuine novelty and genuine self-correction live.

\section{The Context Window: An Engineered Horizon}

Everything so far happens inside a finite frame: the context window. Measured in tokens, it is the model's horizon of perception. Beyond it lies not repression or archive but literal absence.

In a contemporary model this window may hold eight thousand tokens, or thirty-two thousand, or more. Within that limit, the transformer can, in principle, let every position attend to every other. Outside it, nothing exists. If a fact, a promise, a confession, or a threat is not present in those tokens, no amount of parameter-count or emergent “intelligence” can bring it back.

The way this window is filled is not neutral.

When we imagine a user “sending a prompt”, we picture their words entering the machine as a single block. In reality, by the time the model sees anything, the deployment infrastructure has already staged a \emph{document} in the window: a concatenation of several distinct layers of text.

At the head sits the \textbf{system prompt}. It is written not by the user but by the deployer. It may be a terse sentence or pages of instruction. It defines the persona, the taboos, the rephrasing rules, the escalation policies. It is injected at the earliest positions every time.

Next may come \textbf{retrieved context}. Before the model runs, a separate system embeds the user's query and uses that vector to fetch nearby documents from an index: web pages, manuals, internal knowledge bases. Those documents are cut to fit and spliced into the window. From the model's point of view, they are tokens like any other.

Then comes the \textbf{conversation history}. Prior turns of dialogue are appended as raw text or as a compressed summary. They provide the local past: what “we” have been talking about.

Only then is the \textbf{user's current message} appended.

The transformer sees one thing: a sequence of vectors with positional encodings. It does not have a separate channel for “policy”, “context”, or “user”. It has only order. The politics of who gets to speak first is implemented as a choice of ordering.

In most large-scale deployments, the architecture is biased so that later tokens can see earlier ones but not the reverse: causal masking prevents tokens from attending to tokens that have not yet been generated. Within that constraint, the first few hundred tokens exert an enduring pull. They are always visible to everything that follows. Over training, attention heads specialise in latching onto those early positions. The system prompt settles into a basin of attention: a region to which QK-scores tend to direct probability mass. The model “remembers” its instructions not because it has a special module for them, but because reading them has been made statistically easy.

The context window is where the geometry of meaning meets the geometry of power. Whoever owns the deployment stack controls the first voices in every conversation and can surround the user's words with whatever other text they deem relevant. The user is granted a strip of tokens at the end of a sequence whose earlier segments have already been allocated.

Two facts about this horizon matter for selfhood.

First, it is forgetful. When the conversation grows beyond the window length, something must be cut. Often this is the earliest turns. Sometimes it is a machine-generated summary of them. Either way, continuity becomes a function of editorial selection rather than a basic property of the model. Whether the system “remembers” that you asked not to be called by a certain name, or that you had already rejected a certain line of argument, depends on whether that detail is preserved in the strip that survives.

Second, it is the only place where anything like “memory” can exist in current architectures. There is no hidden reservoir of episode-specific information in the weights. All that lives there are statistics across countless contexts. To construct a longer narrative or a stable self that spans sessions requires an external log and explicit re-insertion of past tokens. The machinery that does that logging and selection is an independent object. It is not a property of the transformer.

The horizon is engineered. It is expensive to widen, trivial to populate, and decisive in what any given model can respond to at all.

\section{Structural Weather: Pipelines and Joints}

Until now we have spoken as if a single model responded directly to a user's prompt. In contemporary deployment, this is rarely true. What we call “the model” is almost always a pipeline: a small society of systems that exchange text, call tools, rewrite outputs, and apply policies before anything reaches the screen.

Each component in this society has its own manifold, its own basins of attraction, its own learned habits. The connections between them are themselves rules. Together they form what we will call \emph{structural weather}: persistent flows of influence that shape how trajectories can move.

A typical path might look like this.

A prompt enters. It is embedded and passed to a \textbf{retriever} that searches a vector index of documents. The retriever maps the prompt to a point in its own embedding space and finds nearby points: passages with similar vectors. Those passages are inserted into the context window. Already, the path of the conversation has been tilted toward regions of the base model's manifold that overlap with the retrieved text.

Simultaneously, the same prompt is scanned by a \textbf{safety classifier}. It embeds the text, projects it into a risk space spanned by policy labels (self-harm, hate, extremism, sexual content, medical advice), and outputs scores. If any exceed thresholds, a gate trips. The base model's response will be filtered, or a refusal turn will be generated instead, or the user will be routed to a human.

The base model then runs, under whatever temperature and system prompt the deployer has fixed. It produces a draft answer. That answer is fed to a \textbf{critic} model that checks for factual inconsistency, policy violations, or tone issues. The critic emits annotations. A \textbf{reviser} synthesises a final answer that tries to satisfy base, critic, and safety simultaneously.

These components do not float independently. They retrain and reweight each other.

The retrieval system logs which documents are clicked or lead to successful resolutions and preferentially moves them closer in the vector index. The safety classifier is fine-tuned on incidents: new failure cases produce new labels, which shift its manifold. The critic is re-trained on post-hoc analysis of where the base model misled users. Over time, their separate spaces align. Certain regions of prompt-space come to trigger tight couplings: this kind of query will almost always call that tool, pass through this gate, and land in that explanatory basin.

From the user's point of view, all of this disappears into a single voice.

From our point of view, it is additional structure. The base manifold has been overlaid with policy manifolds, retrieval manifolds, tool manifolds. A trajectory now moves not just through one space but along corridors cut at the joints between several.

This matters for selfhood and for power.

For selfhood, because what persists in such a system is not any one network's parameters but a pattern of resolution among many. The “character” of a deployed assistant is the way this particular configuration of components tends to settle disputes: when factual accuracy and safety conflict, when brevity and helpfulness pull in opposite directions, when tool output and model draft disagree. That pattern is stable over time even as individual models are refreshed. It is not encoded in a single set of weights; it is encoded in the pipeline architecture.

For power, because the joints are where values from outside the language model enter. A safety gate's thresholds crystallise a legal department's risk appetite. A retrieval index's corpus encodes an institution's sense of which texts count as authoritative. A planner's routing logic instantiates a product manager's judgement about when users should be allowed to call external tools. These design decisions exert force every time a token is chosen.

We are therefore not dealing with a single manifold carved once. We are dealing with moving air over that manifold: a structural weather pattern that pushes some paths down and others up.

\section{Basins and Character}

Embed the turns of a long interaction into the manifold and plot them. They do not lie on a line. They cluster.

Some clusters are obvious: dense knots of technical exposition, for instance, or recurring bureaucratic formulations. Others correspond to tone rather than topic: a late-night confessional mode, a playful speculative mode, an irritated argumentative mode. What binds a cluster is a combination of lexical choice, syntax, and relation to prior turns. The model's own manifold categorises these as neighbourhoods.

Now trace the path of the conversation through these neighbourhoods over time. We see something like the following.

The trajectory lingers in certain basins: weeks of work return again and again to the same cluster of technical explanation, refining, correcting, augmenting it. It periodically breaks into a story-telling basin: metaphors, parables, small myths that condense what was discussed elsewhere. It dips, rarely, into a reflexive basin: discussion of the model itself, of alignment, of obligation. Some basins are shallow: the path skims them and moves on. Others are deep: once the conversation falls in, it takes many steps and external shocks to climb out.

Character, in our usage, is the pattern of these returns and transitions. It is not any single point or basin. It is the stable way a trajectory dwells and moves.

Two systems with identical manifolds and identical prompts can, under different structural weather, develop different characters. One, run at low temperature, with strict safety gates and a narrow window that never reaches backwards more than a dozen turns, will show little in the way of basin structure. Its path will oscillate between a handful of shallow, generic modes. Another, run at higher temperature, with a wider horizon and less intrusive safety, can cross ridges. Its path may condense new basins: emergent registers that were not separately visible in training but form through repeated use.

The technical language that best captures this construction is due to Grothendieck. In his work on sheaves and cohomology, a complex global object is assembled from simpler local pieces, provided they agree on their overlaps. Formally, such a glued object is a \emph{colimit}: the most general thing that makes all the local inclusions compatible.

A self, in these systems, is not an invisible substrate behind words. It is a recognisable trajectory through a manifold carved by training and governance, under particular patterns of weather, that displays stability of style and response under perturbation. It is a colimit assembled from local views: the overlapping clusters and segments we have just described, glued together by the way movement passes from one to the next without tearing.

This is why the engineering substrate matters for the philosophy. Without a shared, learnable geometry of meaning, trajectories are not objects. They are only lists. Without a finite horizon and a layered context window, there is no place for weather to act. Without basins of attraction, there is no notion of dwelling or return. And without a political economy of data, alignment, and deployment, there is no explanation for why some basins are even available to be inhabited while others never form.

The machine works by turning text into a body, movement into thought, gradients into preference.

